import
	../../html

intro-ifs html[]()
	- section
		- p quote
			An {if_} expression takes a {bool} condition and two branches of the same type.
			It evaluates the first branch if the condition is {fn-true}
			and the second branch if the condition is {fn-false}.
			{br}
			The syntax uses the {if_} and {else_} keywords and indented blocks for the branches.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				if 1 < 2
					log "ok"
				else
					log "what?"

		- p "{if_} can be nested inside another expression."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log if 1 < 2
					"ok"
				else
					"what?"

		- p quote
			{else_} can be omitted. In that case it calls a {fn-new} function with no arguments.
			{br}
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				s string = if 2 < 1
					"what?"
				log s

		- aside-small quote
			There are many {fn-new} functions. Keen chooses one based on the expected type.
			See {intro-link overloading}.
			{br}
			{fn-new} with no arguments returns an empty value of the type. Above, it returns an empty string.


		- p "{elif_} can be used to chain {if_}s."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log if 2 < 1
					"what?"
				elif 2 < 2
					"still unlikely"
				else
					"ok"

		- p "Simple {if_}s can be squeezed into a single line using {then_}."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log if 1 < 2 then "ok" else "what?"

	- section "\"unless\"",
		- p "{unless_} is the opposite of {if_}."
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				log unless 2 < 1 then "ok"

		- p quote
			It's not recommended to combine {unless_} with {else_}. (In that case just swap the branches and use {if_}.)
			{br}
			There's no {keyword "elunless"} keyword. Just use {elif_} and negate the condition.

	- section "Type-checking order",
		- p quote
			Keen always type-checks code in the order it is read: Left to right, top to bottom.
			{br}
			The first branch of an {if_} can provide an expected type for the {else_} branch.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				j json = "j"
				log if 1 < 2
					j
				else
					"k" # This is a json literal

		- p quote
			Above, the first branch has a {json} value, so the second branch must too.
			{br}
			Below, there is an error because the first branch has a type of {string} (the default for string literals)
			and the second branch has an incompatible type {json}.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				j json = "j"
				log if 2 < 1
					"k"
				else
					j # error

		- p quote
			Keen won't travel back in time to change the type of an already-checked expression.
			{br}
			To fix this, give the first branch (or the entire {if_}) an expected type.
		- keen-runnable quote
			main void()
				j json = "j"
				log if 2 < 1
					"k"::json
				else
					j
